Array.answer:
Binary search. If the target is not found, <code>lo</code> ends up at the correct insert position.
<pre><code>class Solution {
    public int searchInsert(int[] nums, int target) {
        int lo = 0, hi = nums.length - 1;
        while (lo &lt;= hi) {
            int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
            if (nums[mid] == target)
                return mid;
            else if (nums[mid] &lt; target)
                lo = mid + 1;
            else
                hi = mid - 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }
}</code></pre>Time: O(log n)

Assumed ranges: above 90, 81–90, 61–80, 60 and below.
<pre><code>import java.util.*;
class Statistics {
    int x[] = new int[100];
    Scanner ob = new Scanner(System.in);

    void accept() {
        int i;
        for (i = 0; i &lt; 100; i++) {
            System.out.println("Enter marks");
            x[i] = ob.nextInt();
        }
    }

    void statistics() {
        int i, a = 0, b = 0, c = 0, d = 0;
        for (i = 0; i &lt; 100; i++) {
            if (x[i] &gt; 90)
                a++;
            else if (x[i] &gt; 80)
                b++;
            else if (x[i] &gt; 60)
                c++;
            else
                d++;
        }
        System.out.println("Range        No. of students");
        System.out.println("Above 90     " + a);
        System.out.println("80 - 90      " + b);
        System.out.println("60 - 80      " + c);
        System.out.println("Below 60     " + d);
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {
        Statistics s = new Statistics();
        s.accept();
        s.statistics();
    }
}</code></pre>

String.answer:
Try every choice for the first two numbers, then check the rest of the string. The numbers are kept as plain Strings (the input can be 35 digits long, too big for <code>long</code>), so a small helper <code>add()</code> adds two digit-strings using <code>charAt</code>.
<pre><code>class Solution {
    String add(String a, String b) {
        int i = a.length() - 1, j = b.length() - 1, carry = 0;
        String res = "";
        while (i &gt;= 0 || j &gt;= 0 || carry &gt; 0) {
            int sum = carry;
            if (i &gt;= 0) {
                sum = sum + (a.charAt(i) - '0');
                i--;
            }
            if (j &gt;= 0) {
                sum = sum + (b.charAt(j) - '0');
                j--;
            }
            res = (sum % 10) + res;
            carry = sum / 10;
        }
        return res;
    }

    boolean check(String num, int start, String a, String b) {
        int n = num.length();
        while (start &lt; n) {
            String c = add(a, b);
            int len = c.length();
            if (start + len &gt; n)
                return false;
            if (!num.substring(start, start + len).equals(c))
                return false;
            start = start + len;
            a = b;
            b = c;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public boolean isAdditiveNumber(String num) {
        int n = num.length();
        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n / 2; i++) {
            if (num.charAt(0) == '0' &amp;&amp; i &gt; 1)
                break;
            for (int j = i + 1; n - j &gt;= Math.max(i, j - i); j++) {
                if (num.charAt(i) == '0' &amp;&amp; j - i &gt; 1)
                    break;
                String a = num.substring(0, i);
                String b = num.substring(i, j);
                if (check(num, j, a, b))
                    return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}</code></pre>Idea: choose first number = num[0..i), second = num[i..j); then keep generating the next sum and compare it with the following part of the string.

Extract each word character by character. Every time a word is completed, add it to the <b>front</b> of the result.
<pre><code>class Solution {
    public String reverseWords(String s) {
        String res = "", word = "";
        int n = s.length();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (c != ' ') {
                word = word + c;
            } else if (word.length() &gt; 0) {
                if (res.length() == 0)
                    res = word;
                else
                    res = word + " " + res;
                word = "";
            }
        }
        // last word (if the string does not end with a space)
        if (word.length() &gt; 0) {
            if (res.length() == 0)
                res = word;
            else
                res = word + " " + res;
        }
        return res;
    }
}</code></pre>Dry run for "the sky is blue": the → sky the → is sky the → blue is sky the.

Use a String <code>st</code> as a stack: add every opening bracket at the end; when a closing bracket comes, the <b>last</b> character of <code>st</code> must be its matching opening bracket, which is then removed.
<pre><code>class Solution {
    public boolean isValid(String s) {
        String st = "";
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (c == '(' || c == '{' || c == '[') {
                st = st + c;                      // push
            } else {
                if (st.length() == 0)
                    return false;
                char open = st.charAt(st.length() - 1);   // top
                st = st.substring(0, st.length() - 1);    // pop
                if ((c == ')' &amp;&amp; open != '(') ||
                    (c == '}' &amp;&amp; open != '{') ||
                    (c == ']' &amp;&amp; open != '['))
                    return false;
            }
        }
        return st.length() == 0;
    }
}</code></pre>The string is valid only if no opening bracket is left unmatched at the end.

Treat the title as a base-26 number where A=1 ... Z=26. Process left to right: <code>result = result * 26 + value</code>.
<pre><code>class Solution {
    public int titleToNumber(String columnTitle) {
        int result = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; columnTitle.length(); i++) {
            char c = columnTitle.charAt(i);
            result = result * 26 + (c - 'A' + 1);
        }
        return result;
    }
}</code></pre>Dry run for "ZY": 26 → 26*26 + 25 = 701.

A special character is anything that is not a letter or a digit.
<pre><code>class Strength {
    String s;

    Strength(String z) {
        s = z;
    }

    boolean check() {
        int i, l = s.length();
        int uc = 0, lc = 0, d = 0, sp = 0;
        char ch;
        if (l &lt; 12)
            return false;
        for (i = 0; i &lt; l; i++) {
            ch = s.charAt(i);
            if (Character.isUpperCase(ch))
                uc++;
            else if (Character.isLowerCase(ch))
                lc++;
            else if (Character.isDigit(ch))
                d++;
            else
                sp++;
        }
        return uc &gt;= 4 &amp;&amp; lc &gt;= 4 &amp;&amp; d &gt;= 2 &amp;&amp; sp &gt;= 2;
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {
        Strength p = new Strength("HELPmeplz12@#");
        if (p.check())
            System.out.println("Strong password");
        else
            System.out.println("Weak password");
    }
}</code></pre>

Number Based.answer:
Keep increasing <code>i</code> while its square is still less than or equal to x. The square is calculated as a <code>long</code> so it does not overflow for large x.
<pre><code>class Solution {
    public int mySqrt(int x) {
        int i = 1;
        while ((long) i * i &lt;= x) {
            i++;
        }
        return i - 1;
    }
}</code></pre>Dry run for x = 8: 1, 2 satisfy i*i &lt;= 8; 3*3 = 9 stops the loop, so the answer is 3 - 1 = 2.
For x = 0: 1*1 &gt; 0, so the loop does not run and 1 - 1 = 0 is returned.
